# meson: use zero-init-padding-bits=unions to clear unions
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/46600 state=merged
# Starting with gcc 15, union foo f = {0}; only sets the first member of foo to zero,
leaving any padding bytes uninitialized. The pattern is used a lot in mesa, so
instead of fixing each individually, we restore the old behaviour via compiler flag.

Found this in asahi:
Each application is compiling & caching some precompiled shaders on startup, every time under a different cache key, even though the shaders are the same.
Turns out that only the first byte of the asahi_shader_key union is zeroed on gcc >= 15, the remaining are whatever is on the stack right now.
--- meson.build
+++ meson.build
@@ -1435,6 +1435,7 @@ else
     '-Qunused-arguments',
     '-fno-common',
     '-Wno-unknown-pragmas',
+    '-fzero-init-padding-bits=unions',
     # Clang
     '-Wno-microsoft-enum-value',
     '-Wno-unused-function',
@@ -1457,6 +1458,7 @@ else
     # For now we disable this optimization.
     '-flifetime-dse=1',
     '-Wno-unknown-pragmas',
+    '-fzero-init-padding-bits=unions',
     # Clang
     '-Wno-microsoft-enum-value',
     '-fansi-escape-codes',

